title: It is messy when two minds make the same decisions unseen by each other, when what all know crowds out what one knows, and when agreement is taken for truth.
pid: 665430d0-029d-4d79-9171-d377d9f47e73
body: |2-
  Read: actions carry implicit decisions, and two agents' conflict
  (Cognition, 2025); every multi-agent variant made a sequential
  planning task 39 to 70% worse, and agents working apart amplified
  errors 17 times, against 4 under one coordinator (Kim et al. 2025);
  "inter-agent misalignment" is one of three classes of failure in 14
  modes (Cemri et al. 2025); debate between models improves some
  reasoning (Du et al. 2023) but does not reliably beat sampling one
  model several times (Smit et al. 2024). Read in summaries: conceptual
  integrity needs a design from one mind or a few, and n people have
  n(n-1)/2 channels (Brooks 1975); groups discuss what all hold far
  more than what one holds (Stasser and Titus 1985); a misinformed
  Delphi panel may "only add confidence to their ignorance".
  Ours, from the first use: Libby found that two builders of Vic's
  plan would each have invented the wire form between them; the plan
  then gave it whole, and nothing conflicted. Inferred: specialists
  here share one model, so their agreement is weak evidence, and a
  debate between them costs a mission a turn.
